                },
                {
                    "file": "src/net/addr/hostname.cpp",
                    "line": 26,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Abhilash Raju",
                        "email": "abhilash.kollam@gmail.com",
                        "username": "abhilashraju"
                    },
                    "message": "can we do something like this \n```\nbool isValid(const std::string& hostname) {\n    if (hostname.empty() || hostname.length() > 253) return false;\n\n    std::regex pattern(R\"(^(([a-zA-Z0-9]|[a-zA-Z0-9][a-zA-Z0-9\\-]{0,61}[a-zA-Z0-9])\\.)*([A-Za-z0-9]|[A-Za-z0-9][a-zA-Z0-9\\-]{0,61}[a-zA-Z0-9])$)\");\n\n    return std::regex_match(hostname, pattern);\n}\n```"
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/net/addr/hostname.cpp",
                    "line": 26,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "priyair",
                        "email": "priya_irudayaraj@yahoo.co.in",
                        "username": "priyair"
                    },
                    "message": "used FSM instead"
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/net/addr/hostname.cpp",
                    "line": 37,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "William A. Kennington III",
                        "email": "wak@google.com",
                        "username": "wak-google"
                    },
                    "message": "This entire function should never have to allocate, we should be able to do something like above with a regex or some other FSM.\n\nIdeally though I'd prefer we not regex for this, we can write the expression up front and don't need the complexity.\n\nHeck, gemini gave me a pretty good impl that appears to follow the exact rules from RFC1123\n```\n#include <string_view>\n#include <cstddef>\n\n// Standard std::isalnum is not guaranteed constexpr and is locale-dependent.\n// Using custom traits ensures predictable, non-allocating compile-time execution.\nconstexpr bool is_alpha(char c) noexcept {\n    return (c >= 'a' && c <= 'z') || (c >= 'A' && c <= 'Z');\n}\n\nconstexpr bool is_digit(char c) noexcept {\n    return (c >= '0' && c <= '9');\n}\n\nconstexpr bool is_alnum(char c) noexcept {\n    return is_alpha(c) || is_digit(c);\n}\n\n[[nodiscard]] constexpr bool is_valid_fqdn(std::string_view fqdn) noexcept {\n    // RFC 1035: Total FQDN length cannot exceed 253 characters.\n    if (fqdn.empty() || fqdn.length() > 253) {\n        return false;\n    }\n\n    enum class State {\n        Start,      // Initial state, or expecting the first char of a new label\n        LabelChar,  // Processing valid alphanumeric characters in a label\n        Hyphen,     // Just processed a hyphen\n        Dot         // Just processed a dot separator\n    };\n\n    State state = State::Start;\n    std::size_t label_len = 0;\n    bool has_dot = false;\n\n    for (char c : fqdn) {\n        if (c == '.') {\n            // Fails if: starts with dot, consecutive dots (empty label), or label ends in hyphen.\n            if (state == State::Start || state == State::Dot || state == State::Hyphen) {\n                return false;\n            }\n            state = State::Dot;\n            label_len = 0;\n            has_dot = true;\n        } \n        else if (c == '-') {\n            // Fails if: label starts with a hyphen.\n            if (state == State::Start || state == State::Dot) {\n                return false;\n            }\n            state = State::Hyphen;\n            if (++label_len > 63) return false; // RFC 1035 max label length\n        } \n        else if (is_alnum(c)) {\n            state = State::LabelChar;\n            if (++label_len > 63) return false;\n        } \n        else {\n            // Reject any illegal characters (spaces, underscores, special symbols)\n            return false;\n        }\n    }\n\n    // A valid FQDN cannot end in a hyphen.\n    if (state == State::Hyphen) {\n        return false;\n    }\n\n    // A trailing dot is permitted in FQDNs (representing the root).\n    // An FQDN practically requires at least one dot to distinguish from a local hostname.\n    return has_dot || state == State::Dot; \n}\n```"
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/net/addr/hostname.cpp",
                    "line": 37,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "William A. Kennington III",
                        "email": "wak@google.com",
                        "username": "wak-google"
                    },
                    "message": "If you want to allow local hostnames like (localhost), we just need to drop all instances of `has_dot`"
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/net/addr/hostname.cpp",
                    "line": 37,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Abhilash Raju",
                        "email": "abhilash.kollam@gmail.com",
                        "username": "abhilashraju"
                    },
                    "message": "I thought regex would improve the readability. If performance is the reason, then we can make pattern object static, which reduces compilation time.Either way is ok for me."
                }
            ],
